// Class 12 English Core - Flamingo Prose Chapter 3: Deep Water
// Fully standardized to English Core Blueprint & AGENTS.md guidelines

export const c12EngF3HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; color: #E2E8F0; max-width: 950px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 12px; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n\n  <!-- Chapter Header Badge -->\n  <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.22), rgba(56, 239, 125, 0.1)); border: 1.5px solid #11998E; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 22px rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.15);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #11998E; color: #000; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 800; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 6px; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">FLAMINGO &bull; PROSE CHAPTER 3</span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600;\">CLASS XII ENGLISH CORE</span>\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 26px; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 6px 0; letter-spacing: -0.5px;\">Deep Water</h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 15px; margin: 0; font-style: italic;\">\n      By William Douglas (1898&ndash;1980) &mdash; Autobiographical Memoir from <i>Of Men and Mountains</i>: The Psychological Conquest of Paralyzing Hydrophobia\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Quick Glossary & Chapter Dossier Card -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.92); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 12px 0; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📖</span> Quick Glossary &amp; Chapter Dossier: Deep Water\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Author:</b> William O. Douglas (US Supreme Court Justice for 36 years; avid outdoorsman)</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Autobiographical Source:</b> <i>Of Men and Mountains</i> (1950)</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Hydrophobia:</b> Chronic psychological terror of deep water originating from childhood traumas</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">First Trauma:</b> California surf knocking him down at age 3&ndash;4 with his father</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Second Trauma:</b> YMCA Pool Yakima &mdash; tossed into 9-foot deep end by an 18-year-old bully</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Pedagogical Turn:</b> Seven months of systematic, piece-by-piece training with an instructor</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Solo Conquests:</b> Lake Wentworth (2 miles) and Warm Lake in High Cascades</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Roosevelt's Dictum:</b> <i>\"All we have to fear is fear itself\"</i> &mdash; The core philosophical thesis</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Section 1: Executive Summary & Thematic Context -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    1. Executive Summary &amp; Autobiographical Framework\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Deep Water</b>, excerpted from his autobiographical masterpiece <i>Of Men and Mountains</i>, William O. Douglas provides an intensely candid and psychological account of his lifelong battle against a debilitating fear of water. Serving for 36 years as an Associate Justice of the United States Supreme Court, Douglas was renowned for his judicial courage; yet in this personal narrative, he reveals that his greatest internal battle was conquering the childhood terror that paralyzed his senses and ruined his passion for the outdoors.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    The narrative details how a casual childhood wave in California and a near-fatal misadventure at the <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">YMCA pool in Yakima</b> spawned a severe phobia. Through sheer determination, scientific and incremental training under a professional instructor, and solitary tests in glacial mountain lakes, Douglas dismantled his terror brick by brick. His triumph illustrates President Franklin D. Roosevelt's profound wartime axiom: <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">\"All we have to fear is fear itself.\"</b>\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- Section 2: Chronological Narrative Flow -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    2. Deep Narrative Progression: From Terror to Absolute Mastery\n  </h2>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(i) Genesis of Childhood Trauma: California Beach &amp; The YMCA Pool</h3>\n  <p>\n    Douglas’s aversion to water commenced early in his youth. When he was three or four years old, his father took him to a beach in California. As they stood together in the surging surf, an enormous breaker crashed over young Douglas, burying him in roaring foam. His breath departed, and deep terror was etched into his subconscious, while his father stood by laughing, unaware of the lasting emotional scar.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    At age ten or eleven, Douglas resolved to overcome this lingering aversion by learning to swim at the <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Y.M.C.A. pool in Yakima</b>. His mother continually warned him against swimming in the treacherous Yakima River, repeatedly detailing every drowning that occurred in its swift currents. By contrast, the YMCA pool offered safe confines: it was only two to three feet deep at the shallow end, gradually sloping down to nine feet at the other. Equipped with brand new water wings, Douglas paddled along the edge, copying other swimmers and beginning to recover his confidence.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(ii) The Horrifying Misadventure: Tossed into the Nine-Foot Abyss</h3>\n  <p>\n    One afternoon, Douglas arrived at the pool before anyone else. The place was silent, the tiled bottom gleaming white like a bathtub. Hesitant to plunge into the still water alone, he sat on the edge waiting for others. Suddenly, an eighteen-year-old <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">\"big bruiser of a boy\"</b> with rippling muscles on his arms approached. Grinning with casual cruelty, he yelled, <i>\"Hi, Skinny! How’d you like to be ducked?\"</i> and hurled Douglas straight into the nine-foot deep end.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Douglas landed in a sitting posture, swallowed gulps of chlorine water, and sank rapidly to the bottom. Though terror-stricken, he did not lose his reasoning faculties: on the way down, he planned that the moment his feet struck the tiled floor, he would make an enormous spring, shoot to the surface like a cork, lie flat on his stomach, and paddle to the edge.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    However, the descent seemed interminable; the nine feet felt like ninety. When his feet struck bottom, he summoned every ounce of muscular strength and vaulted upward. But instead of rocketing to the surface, he ascended agonizingly slowly. When his eyes broke through, he saw only murky, yellow water. He flailed frantically, swallowed more water, and choked. He attempted to bring his legs up to kick, but they hung dead, rigid, and paralyzed. A terrifying downward suction dragged him back into the watery abyss.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    On his third descent, all frantic struggle subsided. A profound, unnatural tranquility took over; his legs felt limp and leaden, and dark oblivion swept across his consciousness. The panic and terror vanished, replaced by a soothing drowsiness, like floating peacefully into space wrapped in mother's arms. As Douglas crossed into unconsciousness, <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">\"the curtain of life fell.\"</b>\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(iii) Ruined Joys and the Decision to Reclaim Life</h3>\n  <p>\n    Douglas woke up lying on his stomach beside the pool, vomiting water, while the boy who had thrown him murmured sheepishly, <i>\"I was only fooling.\"</i> For years afterward, the psychological shock crippled Douglas. The haunting terror gripped him whenever he went near water &mdash; whether wading through the Tieton or Bumping River, or bathing in Warm Lake in the Cascades.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    The phobia severely restricted his life: it ruined his beloved fishing excursions for landlocked salmon and trout in the Metolius and Deschutes rivers, and denied him the delights of canoeing, boating, and swimming. Unable to tolerate this lifelong handicap, he resolved in October to hire a professional swimming instructor.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(iv) Piece by Piece: The Instructor's Systematic Pedagogy</h3>\n  <p>\n    The instructor adopted an incremental, desensitizing methodology, building a swimmer <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">\"piece by piece\"</b>:\n  </p>\n  <ul style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; padding-left: 20px;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Belt and Pulley Apparatus:</b> The instructor attached a belt around Douglas's waist, connected to a rope running through an overhead pulley on an iron cable. Back and forth across the pool they practiced for three months, gradually extinguishing panic.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Respiratory Rhythm:</b> The instructor trained him to immerse his face underwater and exhale, and lift his nose above the surface to inhale. Practiced hundreds of times, this eradicated the suffocating choking reflex.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Leg Kicking &amp; Relaxation:</b> Holding onto the pool's side, Douglas kicked his legs relentlessly for weeks. Initially, his legs froze in rigid resistance; gradually, they relaxed and yielded to his conscious commands.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>\n    After seven months of rigorous, isolated drills, the instructor integrated these separate skills into a unified swimming stroke, instructing Douglas to dive off and swim lengths. The instructor was finished, but Douglas knew his own psychological trial was not yet over.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(v) Solo Testing &amp; Absolute Conquest</h3>\n  <p>\n    To guarantee that no latent residue of panic remained, Douglas tested himself without supervision. In July, he travelled to <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Lake Wentworth in New Hampshire</b>, dived off the dock at Triggs Island, and swam two miles across open water to Stamp Act Island. When the phantom of old terror briefly flickered in the middle of the lake, Douglas laughed aloud: <i>\"Well, Mr. Terror, what do you think you can do to me?\"</i> &mdash; and the fear vanished.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    To eliminate every lingering doubt, he travelled to Conrad Meadows, camped beside <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Warm Lake</b> in the High Cascades, and swam across to the far shore and back without hesitation. Reaching the shore, he let out a triumphant shout that echoed off Gilbert Peak: he had finally and completely conquered his terror of water.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- Section 3: Literary Devices Master Table -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    3. Literary &amp; Rhetorical Devices Master Table\n  </h2>\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; text-align: left; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.25); color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Literary Device</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Textual Quote / Example</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Thematic Function &amp; Critical Impact</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #11998E;\">Personification</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"I laughed and said, 'Well, Mr. Terror, what do you think you can do to me?'\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Transforms abstract phobia into an external, tangible antagonist that can be defied, mocked, and defeated.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #11998E;\">Metaphor</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"The curtain of life fell.\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Compares biological loss of consciousness and near-death to the final closing curtain of a theatrical play.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #11998E;\">Simile</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"I would bob to the surface like a cork.\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Emphasizes his youthful, overly optimistic plan of shooting effortlessly to the top of the pool.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #11998E;\">Sensory Imagery</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"Lungs ached, head throbbed, dirty yellow water with a dark tinge.\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Evokes visceral somatic sensations of suffocation, severe ear pressure, panic, and sensory blindness.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #11998E;\">Historical Allusion</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"All we have to fear is fear itself.\" &mdash; Franklin D. Roosevelt</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Elevates a personal triumph over phobia into a universal philosophical thesis on human willpower and liberation.</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Section 4: Philosophical Core -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    4. Philosophical Core: Roosevelt's Dictum &amp; Existential Triumph\n  </h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.1); border-left: 5px solid #11998E; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 15px; color: #F1F5F9; font-style: italic; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      \"In death there is peace. There is terror only in the fear of death, as Roosevelt knew when he said, 'All we have to fear is fear itself.' Because I had experienced both the sensation of dying and the terror that fear of it can produce, the will to live somehow grew in intensity.\"\n    </p>\n    <span style=\"display: block; margin-top: 8px; color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&mdash; William O. Douglas, Deep Water</span>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c12EngF3HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; color: #E2E8F0; max-width: 950px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 12px; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n\n  <!-- Solutions Header Badge -->\n  <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.22), rgba(56, 239, 125, 0.1)); border: 1.5px solid #11998E; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 22px rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.15);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #11998E; color: #000; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 800; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 6px; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">QUESTION BANK &bull; CHAPTER 3</span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600;\">CLASS XII ENGLISH CORE</span>\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 6px 0;\">Deep Water &mdash; Complete Board Solutions</h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin: 0;\">\n      Standardized for CBSE &amp; JKBOSE • High-Scoring Extract, Short, Long Thematic &amp; Official NCERT Solutions\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: EXTRACT-BASED QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION A: Extract-Based Questions (16 Questions | 4 Extracts)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Extract 1 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"It had happened when I was ten or eleven years old. I had decided to learn to swim. There was a pool at the Y.M.C.A. in Yakima that offered exactly the opportunity. The Yakima River was treacherous. Mother continually warned against it, and kept fresh in my mind the details of each drowning in the river. But the Y.M.C.A. pool was safe. It was only two or three feet deep at the shallow end; and while it was nine feet deep at the other, the drop was gradual.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q1: Why did Douglas choose the YMCA pool in Yakima instead of the Yakima River?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Yakima River was dangerous with swift currents and frequent drownings, whereas the YMCA pool was secure, shallow at one end, and featured a gradual slope.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q2: How did Douglas's mother keep the dread of the river vivid in his consciousness?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> She continually warned him and recounted graphic details of every individual who drowned in the Yakima River.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q3: What gear did Douglas acquire to build his swimming confidence at the pool?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> A pair of inflatable water wings to help him paddle and imitate other boys safely.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q4: Which word in the extract denotes 'perilous, deceptive, and unpredictable'?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> 'Treacherous'.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 2 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"I went down, down, endlessly. I opened my eyes. Nothing but water with a yellow glow &mdash; dark water that one could not see through. And then sheer, stark terror seized me, terror that knows no understanding, terror that knows no control, terror that no one can understand who has not experienced it. I was shrieking under water. I was paralyzed under water &mdash; stiff, rigid with fear. Even the screams in my throat were frozen.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q5: What visual perception did Douglas have as he sank downwards?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> He saw nothing but impenetrable dark water with an eerie, dirty yellow glow.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q6: How does Douglas describe the nature of 'stark terror'?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> As an absolute force that defies understanding and control, incomprehensible to anyone who has not experienced it firsthand.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q7: What physical paralysis gripped his body underwater?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> His legs became stiff and rigid with fear, and the vocal screams in his throat froze into silence.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q8: Identify the rhetorical device in the repeated phrase \"terror that knows no...\".</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Anaphora / Repetition, used to evoke the all-consuming, overwhelming intensity of absolute dread.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 3 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"Then all effort ceased. I relaxed. Even my legs felt limp; and a blackness swept over my brain. It wiped out fear; it wiped out terror. There was no more panic. It was quiet and peace. Nothing to be afraid of. This is nice... to be drowsy... to go to sleep... no need to jump... too tired to jump... it’s nice to be carried gently... to float along in space... tender arms around me... tender arms like mother’s... now I must go to sleep... I crossed to oblivion, and the curtain of life fell.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q9: Why did all physical effort suddenly cease for Douglas?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Severe oxygen starvation and utter physical exhaustion overpowered his nervous system, causing his body to surrender.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q10: What psychological state replaced panic and terror?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> A tranquil, painless calm, deep drowsiness, and comforting oblivion resembling sleep in maternal arms.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q11: Identify the metaphorical expression used to describe entering near-death unconsciousness.</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> <i>\"The curtain of life fell\"</i>, comparing the end of conscious life to the conclusion of a theatrical drama.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q12: Where did Douglas find himself when he regained consciousness?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Lying on his stomach beside the pool, vomiting water, while his rescuer and bystander argued nearby.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 4 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"Thus, piece by piece, he built a swimmer. And when he had perfected each piece, he put them together into an integrated whole. In April he said, 'Now you can swim. Dive off and swim the length of the pool, crawl stroke.' I did. The instructor was finished. But I was not finished.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q13: Who is 'he' referred to in this extract?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The dedicated professional swimming instructor hired by Douglas in October.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q14: Explain the phrase 'piece by piece'.</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Teaching swimming incrementally &mdash; first harness support, then breathing techniques, then leg kicking, before integrating them.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q15: Why did Douglas say, 'The instructor was finished. But I was not finished'?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Because while the instructor's mechanical training was complete, Douglas needed to test his internal psychological mastery over terror alone.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 15px;\">Q16: In which month did the instructor declare Douglas ready to swim?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> In the month of April, after seven months of disciplined instruction.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (3 Marks Each | 40&ndash;50 Words)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q17 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q17: What early childhood incident at the California beach first seeded Douglas's aversion to water?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> At age three or four, Douglas stood in the California surf holding his father's hand. An enormous wave knocked him down and swept over him. Buried underwater with breath knocked out, lasting terror took root in his heart, even while his father casually laughed.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q18 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q18: What strategy did Douglas formulate as he sank towards the bottom of the YMCA pool?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Douglas planned that as soon as his feet struck the tiled floor, he would summon all muscular force to make a mighty spring, bob to the surface like a cork, lie flat on his stomach, and paddle smoothly to the pool edge.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q19 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q19: Why did Douglas's survival plan fail when he struck the pool bottom?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The nine-foot depth felt like ninety. When he jumped with all his power, he rose agonizingly slowly instead of popping up like a cork. Surfacing partially, he swallowed suffocating yellow water, choked, and found his stiffened legs completely paralyzed.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q20 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q20: How did the near-drowning misadventure ruin Douglas's outdoor joys for years?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The traumatic terror incapacitated him whenever he approached water. It ruined his cherished fishing expeditions for landlocked salmon and trout in the Metolius and Deschutes rivers, and deprived him of canoeing, boating, and recreational swimming.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q21 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q21: How did the swimming instructor employ the belt-and-pulley apparatus?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The instructor tied a belt around Douglas's waist attached to a rope running across an overhead cable via a pulley. For three months, Douglas traversed the pool held securely, systematically desensitizing him from panic without the danger of sinking.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q22 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q22: How did the instructor train Douglas in breathing underwater?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The instructor commanded Douglas to submerge his face underwater and exhale, then raise his nose above water to inhale. Repeated hundreds of times, this drill eliminated the violent choking panic associated with submersion.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q23 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q23: How did Douglas overcome the paralysis of his legs while swimming?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Douglas gripped the edge of the pool and kicked his legs continuously for weeks. Initially, his legs hung frozen and refused to obey; gradually, through persistent effort, they relaxed and yielded to his conscious commands.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q24 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q24: Why was Douglas dissatisfied even after the instructor completed his training?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Douglas feared that residual panic would seize him when swimming alone without the instructor's supportive presence. He demanded self-verified proof of psychological conquest in deep, natural waters.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q25 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q25: How did Douglas challenge 'Mr. Terror' at Lake Wentworth?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Douglas dived off Triggs Island in Lake Wentworth, New Hampshire, swimming two miles to Stamp Act Island. When the phantom of old fear stirred mid-lake, he laughed and defied it: <i>\"Well, Mr. Terror, what do you think you can do to me?\"</i> &mdash; and it vanished.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q26 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q26: How did Douglas confirm his final victory over water at Warm Lake?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Douglas hiked to Warm Lake in the High Cascades, dived in, and swam across to the far shore and back without hesitation. Emerging triumphant, he shouted with joy, and Gilbert Peak echoed his triumph, marking complete liberation.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q27 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q27: What physical sensations accompanied Douglas's passage into unconsciousness?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> As his struggles ceased, all pain, terror, and trembling vanished. His limbs felt limp, and a soothing darkness enveloped his brain, producing a tranquil sensation of drifting effortlessly through space wrapped in maternal tenderness.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q28 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q28: What profound philosophical insight did Douglas draw from his brush with death?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> He realized that in biological death there is absolute peace; terror exists only in the anticipatory fear of death. Having experienced both near-death and fear, his passion and will to live grew exponentially in intensity.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LONG ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION C: Long Answer Questions (6 Marks Each | 120&ndash;150 Words)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q29 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q29: Describe the terrifying near-death ordeal of William Douglas in the YMCA pool and his psychological journey toward oblivion.</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Introduction &amp; Setting:</b><br/>\n        While waiting alone on the edge of the YMCA pool in Yakima, ten-year-old William Douglas was unexpectedly hurled into the nine-foot deep end by an eighteen-year-old bully. Douglas sank rapidly in a sitting posture, swallowing water and descending into dark terror.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">The Struggle &amp; Physical Agony:</b><br/>\n        Though frightened, Douglas remained rational: he planned to spring forcefully from the bottom, float up like a cork, and stroke to safety. However, the nine feet felt like ninety. His upward spring failed to propel him; he surfaced agonizingly slowly, beholding only dirty yellow water. Paralyzing fear gripped him &mdash; his lungs ached, his head throbbed, and his rigid legs hung lifeless. On his second and third descents, he swallowed water and choked, his vocal cords frozen in terror.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Surrender &amp; Oblivion:</b><br/>\n        Eventually, oxygen starvation ended all physical exertion. A peaceful numbness swept across his consciousness, wiping out fear and panic. Feeling as though he were floating through space cradled in tender maternal arms, Douglas crossed peacefully into oblivion as the curtain of life fell.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q30 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q30: \"Piece by piece, he built a swimmer.\" Explain the systematic methodology adopted by the instructor to rebuild Douglas's swimming ability.</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Introduction &amp; Pedagogical Principle:</b><br/>\n        Understanding the deep-seated psychological trauma paralyzing Douglas, the professional swimming instructor adopted a patient, scientific, incremental methodology rather than plunging him directly into deep water.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">The Three-Stage Training:</b><br/>\n        First, the instructor fitted Douglas with a belt attached to a rope running across an overhead pulley on an iron cable. For three months, Douglas traversed the pool safely harnessed, gradually desensitizing him from panic. Second, the instructor trained him in respiratory control: exhaling underwater and inhaling above water, repeated hundreds of times to eliminate the choking reflex. Third, Douglas kicked his legs relentlessly at the pool edge for weeks until his stiff limbs relaxed and responded to command.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Synthesis &amp; Mastery:</b><br/>\n        After seven months, the instructor integrated these isolated mechanics into a harmonious swimming stroke. Through this patient scaffolding, the instructor transformed a traumatized boy into a skilled, competent swimmer.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q31 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q31: How does Douglas's personal victory over hydrophobia illustrate President Roosevelt's statement: \"All we have to fear is fear itself\"?</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Philosophical Context:</b><br/>\n        Douglas’s autobiographical journey provides a living demonstration of President Franklin D. Roosevelt's famous declaration: <i>\"All we have to fear is fear itself.\"</i> Douglas understood this maxim from personal brush with mortal peril.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Analysis of Fear vs. Death:</b><br/>\n        Having sunk into near-death unconsciousness in the YMCA pool, Douglas discovered that biological death brings tranquil peace and absence of pain. The true torment of life lies in anticipatory fear &mdash; the paralyzing dread that imprisons the human spirit. For years, this baseless phobia restricted Douglas's mobility, ruined his fishing expeditions, and robbed him of joy.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Conquest &amp; Liberation:</b><br/>\n        When Douglas resolved to confront his demon directly &mdash; mocking \"Mr. Terror\" at Lake Wentworth and swimming across Warm Lake &mdash; the phantom dissolved. The realization that fear possesses only the power our minds concede liberated him, intensifying his zest for living.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q32 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q32: Trace Douglas's solitary challenges at Lake Wentworth and Warm Lake to eliminate all residual fear.</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">The Need for Self-Verification:</b><br/>\n        Although the instructor completed his training in April, Douglas refused to be complacent. He recognized that swimming under an instructor's watchful eye was fundamentally different from confronting vast, unmonitored open waters alone.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Lake Wentworth &amp; Confronting Terror:</b><br/>\n        In July, Douglas traveled to Lake Wentworth in New Hampshire. Diving off Triggs Island, he swam two miles across the lake using crawl, breaststroke, and backstroke. In the middle of the lake, when he put his face underwater and saw only bottomless water, the miniature phantom of fear flared up. Instead of panicking, Douglas laughed and mocked: <i>\"Well, Mr. Terror, what do you think you can do to me?\"</i> &mdash; and the fear vanished immediately.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Warm Lake &amp; Ultimate Triumph:</b><br/>\n        To eradicate any lingering vestige of doubt, he hiked to Warm Lake in the High Cascades, swam to the opposite shore and back. Shouting with ecstasy as Gilbert Peak echoed his cry, Douglas achieved total, permanent mastery over water.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q33 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q33: How does Douglas depict the psychological mechanics of terror and physical paralysis in water?</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Sensory Deprivation &amp; Distorted Perception:</b><br/>\n        Douglas vividly portrays how acute fear warps spatial and physical perception. When submerged, the nine feet felt like ninety, and daylight was replaced by a suffocating dirty yellow glare.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Somatic Paralysis:</b><br/>\n        The narrative meticulously charts how psychological terror cascades into physiological paralysis: throat muscles seize, rendering screams silent; the chest thumps violently as lungs fill with chlorine water; and legs transform into leaden weights that refuse conscious neural commands. Douglas compares this to a person wrestling with an irresistible nightmare.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Critical Conclusion:</b><br/>\n        Through raw sensory prose, Douglas deconstructs fear not as an abstract emotion, but as an overwhelming somatic breakdown that can only be undone through disciplined, conscious reconditioning.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q34 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(17, 153, 142, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q34: What universal message does 'Deep Water' convey about human willpower and conquering phobias?</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Universal Human Dilemma:</b><br/>\n        Every individual confronts irrational phobias, deep traumas, or self-limiting psychological boundaries that restrict their potential and ruin their happiness.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">The Recipe for Conquest:</b><br/>\n        Douglas proves that no trauma is insurmountable. Liberation requires: (1) Absolute refusal to surrender to handicaps; (2) Seeking expert, scientific instruction; (3) Breaking daunting challenges into manageable, incremental tasks; and (4) The courage to confront fear directly in solitary environments.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #11998E;\">Critical Conclusion:</b><br/>\n        Conquering fear expands human freedom. Once an individual stares down their deepest terrors, the psychological chains break, allowing them to release their ties and walk paths of unbounded confidence.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION D: NCERT TEXTBOOK EXERCISES -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #11998E; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION D: NCERT Textbook Exercises (Think As You Read &amp; Understanding The Text)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q35 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q35: What is the \"misadventure\" that William Douglas speaks about?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The misadventure took place at the YMCA pool in Yakima when Douglas, sitting alone on the poolside, was picked up and tossed into the nine-foot deep end by an older boy. Unable to swim, Douglas nearly drowned, swallowed water, suffocated, and suffered acute psychological trauma.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q36 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q36: What series of emotions and fears did Douglas experience when thrown into the pool? What plans did he make to surface?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Douglas felt sheer terror but remained mentally alert. He planned to spring from the bottom, float up like a cork, and paddle to the edge. When the plan failed, he experienced suffocating terror, paralyzed limbs, and pounding lungs. On his third failed attempt, panic gave way to peaceful numbness and total oblivion.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q37 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q37: How did this traumatic experience affect Douglas's life?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The near-drowning left him physically weakened, trembling, and unable to eat for days. It instilled a chronic fear of water that persisted for years, ruining his recreational fishing trips, canoeing, and swimming adventures across the Pacific Northwest.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q38 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #11998E;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #11998E; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q38: Why was Douglas determined to overcome his fear of water?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #11998E; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The fear held him in its cruel grip, depriving him of the outdoor joys he cherished &mdash; such as fishing for salmon in the Metolius and Deschutes rivers, and boating in the Cascades. Determined to reclaim his life and joy, he decided to hire an instructor and eliminate the phobia.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

